Note about Fact (5) not being strictly necessary for the proof: Fact (5) is what allows us to claim that our first series construction is greedy, with fastest growth at each step. Without using Fact (5), we would not have a naturally greedy construction as our first series currently is. However, it would ''still'' be true that a construction with <math>\mathbb{F}_p[G] \rtimes H</math> as its first term cannot go all the way to the whole group <math>A</math> in the second term, because <math>H</math> being 2-subnormal but not normal in <math>G</math> means that <math>\mathbb{F}_p[G] \rtimes H</math> is 2-subnormal but not normal in <math>\mathbb{F}_p[G] \rtimes G = A</math>. Thus, completing the first series would still require a second term that is proper in <math>A</math>. Therefore, we'd still get that the second series overtakes the first, establishing that there is no fastest ascending 3-subnormal series. (It's also worth noting that if Fact (5) were actually false, we'd directly get a contradiction from the existence of no fastest way to complete the first series after making the first move selecting <math>\mathbb{F}_p[G] \rtimes H</math>; however, we don't want to go there since we know that Fact (5) is, in fact, true).

{{tabular proof format}}

===Example of a 3-subnormal subgroup with no unique largest normalizing 2-subnormal subgroup containing it===

Let <math>H</math> be a nontrivial subgroup of a finite group <math>G</math> that is a join of two 2-subnormal subgroups <math>H_1,H_2</math> of <math>G</math> but is not itself 2-subnormal (such subgroups exist, see Fact (2)). In symbols, <math>H = \langle H_1 , H_2 \rangle</math>.

Let <math>p</math> be a prime. Let <math>A</math> be the wreath product of the [[group of prime order]] with <math>G</math>. Equivalently, <math>A</math> is the semidirect product of the additive group of <math>\mathbb{F}_p[G]</math> with <math>G</math> acting by left multiplication.

Let <math>B = \mathbb{F}_p[H]</math>. Then, <math>N_A(B) = \mathbb{F}_p[G] \rtimes H</math>. Facts (3) and (4) imply that the subnormal depth of <math>N_A(B)</math> in <math>A</math> equals the subnormal depth of <math>H</math> in <math>G</math>, so by our assumption that <math>H</math> is not 2-subnormal in <math>G</math>, we get that <math>N_A(B)</math> is not 2-subnormal in <math>A</math>.

However, any 2-subnormal subgroup that normalizes <math>B</math> and contains <math>B</math> must be in <math>N_A(B)</math>. Further, we can see that the subgroups:

<math>C_1 = \mathbb{F}_p[G] \rtimes H_1, C_2 = \mathbb{F}_p[G] \rtimes H_2</math>

both are in <math>N_A(B)</math> and 2-subnormal (we again use Facts (3) and (4)). Hence, if there is a unique largest 2-subnormal subgroup of <math>A</math> in <math>N_A(B)</math> it must contain both <math>C_1</math> and <math>C_2</math>. But <math>\langle C_1, C_2 \rangle = N_A(B)</math>, which is not 2-subnormal as established above.
